// endpoint_subscribe_merge_queue.proto — SubscribeMergeQueue: tell one
// workspace's agent about a merge queue's changes as they happen, so it can
// wait on the queue (land after the queue drains, report a landing to the
// user) without polling it. AGENT API section.
//
// DELIVERY: each notice is submitted to the subscriber's session as a
// deferred prompt of origin
// conversation.v1.PromptOrigin.PROMPT_ORIGIN_MERGE_QUEUE_NOTICE, the same
// path an async merge's handback takes: it waits behind a running turn, wakes
// an idle agent, and the feed draws no row for it.
//
// WHAT IS TOLD: a merge put in line, a merge leaving the queue (landed,
// failed, or taken off it: evicted, dequeued, or abandoned under its closing
// workspace or a restart), and a deploy of the daemon's own checkout ending
// (its build running, or failed), naming the reported landings its build
// carries. NOTHING ELSE IS TOLD: not a merge starting or moving to a new step,
// not a reorder, not a pause or a resume, and not a merge a restart cut short
// being put back in line, which never left it. A notice wakes the subscriber
// and costs it a full read of its context, and none of those is a change a
// subscriber waits on. A deploy is the daemon's own, not one queue's, so it is
// told to every subscription, whatever repository it scopes.
//
// A NOTICE IS ONE SHORT LINE: the queue, the change, and how the queue stands
// after it in a few words (how many merges are in line, the running merge and
// its step, whether it is paused). It never lists the queue: `merge-queue
// status` reads it. A notice still waiting behind the subscriber's turn when
// the next change comes is superseded by one that names every change it
// named, then the new one, still on one line.
//
// SCOPE: a workspace holds AT MOST ONE subscription per scope -- one
// repository's queue, or every repository's -- and subscribing again in a
// scope it holds stands in for that subscription. A scoped and an unscoped
// subscription may stand together; a change both admit is delivered once, as
// one notice.
//
// LIFETIME: a subscription ends when its workspace unsubscribes in its scope,
// when the workspace closes, however it closes (closed, killed, nuked, or
// closed by its own landing), and when a queue in its scope DEPLETES -- a
// merge leaves it and no merge is left in line, so nothing runs. Re-opening
// the workspace does not restore it. A depletion ends every subscription in
// that queue's scope, the repository's and the every-repository one alike, so
// its notice is the subscription's LAST and says the subscription has ended.
// Following the queue's next work takes a new subscription; one taken after
// the last notice stands, even one taken before the daemon finished
// delivering it. A deploy ending after the depletion is told only to a
// subscription taken again.
// It is DURABLE across a daemon restart and a handover: a landing's own deploy
// restarts the daemon, so a subscription that a restart dropped would end at
// the very change it exists to report. A change made while no daemon serves
// is never reported; the next notice's few words on the queue carry its
// effect.
//
// NOTHING SLIPS BETWEEN READING AND FOLLOWING. The answer carries the queues
// in scope as they stand when the subscription is taken (MergeQueueStatus),
// and every change after that answer that a subscription is told is noticed.

